{
  "abstract": "rStation Paper 1 (Appendix C.6) proves $\\liminf L(1,\\chi)\\log q\\ge2\\pi^2/9$ for real primitive characters $\\chi$ of conductor $q$. It combines Burgess's estimate, which gives the primitive-ideal count its mean density above $q^{1/4}$, with the prime-sum bound (C1) from Green's determinant, whose main term is $-\\frac12\\log q$. As $q\\to\\infty$ these inputs define a constraint system $S(\\beta,\\theta)$ for a prime measure $\\nu$ and the primitive-ideal measure $\\mu=A\\exp^*(\\nu)$ on the scale $u=\\log n/\\log q$: the prime density is at most $2/u$, $\\mu$ has constant density $x$ above $\\beta$, and the prime mass satisfies (C1) at every scale with coefficient $\\theta$. Paper 1's inputs give $\\beta=1/4$, $\\theta=1/2$. We prove that $x\\ge1/(\\beta+\\theta)$ on $S(\\beta,\\theta)$, with equality attained if and only if $\\beta\\le\\theta/(e-1)$. The equality profile is unique. For $\\beta=1/4$, $\\theta=1/2$ it is the profile that Paper 52 found forced on characters with $L(1,\\chi)\\log q\\to2\\pi^2/9$; the new point is that it meets (C1) at every intermediate scale. So $2\\pi^2/9$ is exact for limit arguments from these inputs, and improving it needs a smaller $\\theta$, a smaller $\\beta$, or an input that the profile violates; the profile has $\\sum_{p\\le q^{1/4-\\varepsilon}}(1+\\chi(p))/p\\to0$. The proof reduces (C1) to the positivity of a solution of $E'(u)=-xE(u-\\beta)$, by a Gronwall argument for $\\beta\\le\\theta/2$ and a renewal construction up to $\\theta/(e-1)$. Above that threshold a stability estimate gives a strict gap, and the minimum is always attained. At the Pólya–Vinogradov exponents $\\beta=\\theta=1/2$ the minimum exceeds $1.1662$, above C.5's $2/\\sqrt3$. Attaining the constant would also fix zeros: if $L(1,\\chi)\\log q\\to2\\pi^2/9$, then $(\\log q/\\zeta(2))L(1+z/\\log q,\\chi)\\to z+\\frac43e^{-z/4}$ uniformly on compact sets, so $L(s,\\chi)$ has real zeros $\\sigma_0$ with $(1-\\sigma_0)\\log q\\to2.4762$ and $6.0485$. These are characteristic roots of the delay equation. Conversely, by Goldfeld's argument, no zero on $[1-4/\\log q,1)$ gives $L(1,\\chi)\\log q\\ge(4/e)\\zeta(2)-o(1)$. Theorem 2 concerns the limiting system only; no finite-conductor construction is given.",
